From: ClauCoDillo NuttX port
Subject: [PATCH] mwin: nuttxkbd modifier/special keys must not alias ASCII

kbd_codec.h's `enum kbd_keycode_e` numbers its special-key constants
sequentially from 0, which puts a good chunk of them in the same
numeric range as printable ASCII: KEYCODE_PAUSE is 32 (' '),
KEYCODE_F1..F12 are 64..75 ('@' and 'A'..'K'), KEYCODE_LCTRL..RGUI are
88..95 ('X'..'_'), etc.

src/drivers/kbd_nuttx_event.c's nuttxkbd_Read() called
translate_keycode() (src/drivers/kbd_nuttx_common.h) on every keyboard
event's raw `code` unconditionally -- but that table is only valid for
the KEYBOARD_SPECPRESS/_SPECREL pair (an actual kbd_keycode_e enum
value, from sim_keyboard.c's is_special dispatch); the ordinary
KEYBOARD_PRESS/_RELEASE pair carries the literal character code
straight from X11's XLookupString(), which can be any value in the
same 0..127 range. So typing an ordinary Space, '@', or any of
'A'..'K' got run through the special-key table anyway, matched one of
its cases by numeric coincidence, and turned into a phantom
Pause/F-key report instead of the actual character -- confirmed live:
typing a capitalized word or a sentence with a space made input look
like it silently "stopped working" partway through.

Separately, translate_keycode()'s own table had no cases at all for
the modifier keys themselves (KEYCODE_LCTRL/RCTRL/LSHIFT/RSHIFT/
LALT/RALT/LGUI/RGUI) despite update_modifiers() right below it already
switching on their MWKEY_* equivalents to track shift/ctrl/alt/meta
state -- so even on the SPEC path, holding either Shift key fell
through to the same `code < 128` default and produced a literal 'Z'
(LSHIFT=90=0x5A) or '[' (RSHIFT=91=0x5B) character instead of a
modifier report.

kbd_nuttx_raw.c's own KBD_PRESS case already keeps ordinary keys off
translate_keycode() entirely, for exactly this reason -- this patch
brings kbd_nuttx_event.c (the driver this port's boards actually use,
CONFIG_MICROWINDOWS_KBD_EVENT) in line with it, and fills in the
missing modifier-key cases.

Found and verified while testing text entry for the ClauCoDillo
browser port: Shift+; produced "Z:" instead of ":", right Shift
produced "[:", and typing anything with a capital A-K or a space
appeared to do nothing. Confirmed fixed live: normal text, Space,
'@', capitalized words, and both Shift keys combined with punctuation
all now produce exactly the typed character.
---
--- a/src/drivers/kbd_nuttx_event.c
+++ b/src/drivers/kbd_nuttx_event.c
@@ -87,20 +87,35 @@
       return KBD_NODATA;
     }
 
-  MWKEY key = translate_keycode(event.code);
-  /* KEYBOARD_PRESS (0) covers ordinary/ASCII keys; special keys
-   * (Backspace, Enter, Delete, arrows, ...) are reported with
-   * KEYBOARD_SPECPRESS (2) or KEYBOARD_SPECREL (3) instead -- see
-   * sim_keyboard.c's own is_special dispatch, which chooses between
-   * the plain and "SPEC" pairs for exactly this reason.
-   * Checking only KEYBOARD_PRESS here meant every special-key press
-   * (event.type == 2) failed this comparison and fell through to the
-   * release branch below -- so did the matching real release
-   * (event.type == 3, also != 0) -- meaning *every* special key
-   * reported "release" for both its actual press and its actual
-   * release, and "press" never happened at all. Confirmed live: a
-   * user could not delete text with Backspace or submit a URL with
-   * Enter -- Fl_Input's own key handling only acts on key-*down*. */
+  /* KEYBOARD_PRESS/_RELEASE (0/1) cover ordinary/ASCII keys, reported
+   * as the literal character code straight from X11's XLookupString()
+   * (see sim_x11eventloop.c); KEYBOARD_SPECPRESS/_SPECREL (2/3) cover
+   * special keys (Backspace, Enter, arrows, Shift/Ctrl/Alt, ...),
+   * reported as a kbd_codec.h `enum kbd_keycode_e` value instead --
+   * see sim_keyboard.c's own is_special dispatch, which chooses
+   * between the plain and "SPEC" pairs for exactly this reason.
+   *
+   * translate_keycode() (kbd_nuttx_common.h) is a table for that
+   * *enum*, valid only for the SPEC pair. Calling it unconditionally
+   * on every event -- as this used to -- fed ordinary character codes
+   * through it too, and the enum's own numbering happens to overlap
+   * printable ASCII: KEYCODE_PAUSE is 32 (' '), KEYCODE_F1..F12 are
+   * 64..75 ('@' and 'A'..'K'), KEYCODE_LSHIFT/RSHIFT are 90/91 ('Z'/
+   * '['), etc. So typing Space, '@', any of 'A'..'K', or holding
+   * either Shift key, silently got hijacked into a phantom
+   * Pause/F-key/modifier report instead of producing (or correctly
+   * suppressing, for Shift itself) a character -- confirmed live: a
+   * capitalized word or a Space mid-sentence made the rest of the
+   * input look like it "stopped working" for that keystroke, and
+   * holding Shift alone inserted a stray 'Z' or '[' before the real,
+   * correctly shift-resolved character. kbd_nuttx_raw.c's own
+   * KBD_PRESS case already keeps ordinary keys on a separate path
+   * from translate_keycode() for exactly this reason -- this brings
+   * kbd_nuttx_event.c in line with it. */
+  int is_special = (event.type == KEYBOARD_SPECPRESS ||
+                     event.type == KEYBOARD_SPECREL);
+  MWKEY key = is_special ? translate_keycode(event.code) :
+              (MWKEY) event.code;
   int press = (event.type == KEYBOARD_PRESS ||
                event.type == KEYBOARD_SPECPRESS);
 

--- a/src/drivers/kbd_nuttx_common.h
+++ b/src/drivers/kbd_nuttx_common.h
@@ -69,6 +69,35 @@
     case KEYCODE_MENU:
       return MWKEY_MENU;
 
+      /* Modifier keys. Their enum values (kbd_codec.h) happen to fall
+       * in the printable-ASCII range (LCTRL=88 'X' ... RGUI=95 '_'),
+       * so without explicit cases here they fell through to the
+       * `code < 128` branch below and got returned as literal ASCII
+       * characters instead of MWKEY modifier codes -- update_modifiers()
+       * already switches on these exact MWKEY_* constants to track
+       * shift/ctrl/alt/meta state, but translate_keycode() never
+       * actually produced them. Confirmed live: holding either Shift
+       * key inserted a spurious 'Z' (LSHIFT=90=0x5A) or '[' (RSHIFT=
+       * 91=0x5B) into the focused text field before the real
+       * (correctly shift-resolved, see sim_x11eventloop.c) character
+       * arrived. */
+    case KEYCODE_LCTRL:
+      return MWKEY_LCTRL;
+    case KEYCODE_RCTRL:
+      return MWKEY_RCTRL;
+    case KEYCODE_LSHIFT:
+      return MWKEY_LSHIFT;
+    case KEYCODE_RSHIFT:
+      return MWKEY_RSHIFT;
+    case KEYCODE_LALT:
+      return MWKEY_LALT;
+    case KEYCODE_RALT:
+      return MWKEY_RALT;
+    case KEYCODE_LGUI:
+      return MWKEY_LMETA;
+    case KEYCODE_RGUI:
+      return MWKEY_RMETA;
+
     default:
       if (code < 128)
         {
